WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.350
Like Infinity War before it, Avengers: Endgame
is a marvel of storytelling, but is the execution

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:06.350
perfect?

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:07.350
Not really.

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:10.630
Throughout its three-hour runtime, Endgame
takes some narrative turns that teeter on

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:14.620
the edge of nonsense, and some of screenwriters'
choices threaten to ruin the movie if you

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:16.010
think about them too hard.

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:19.340
Here are the biggest plot holes in Avengers:
Endgame that everybody just ignored.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:20.340
Strange dissonance

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:24.060
During the film's time heist, Bruce Banner
visits the Sanctum Sanctorum in 2012 New York

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:25.060
City.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:27.890
He's searching for Dr. Strange, to borrow
the time stone he's known to guard.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:31.410
But instead of Strange, he finds the Ancient
One, Strange's mentor, who doesn't meet the

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:34.270
doctor until the events of his movie in 2016.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:37.870
Despite Strange not yet being the Sorcerer
Supreme, the Ancient One has full knowledge

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:41.450
of who he is, and hearing that he gives away
the Time Stone in the future is enough to

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:43.510
convince her to give up her own stone.

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:47.200
That's how much she trusts Strange, saying,
"Strange is meant to be the best of us."

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.130
It's presented as a foregone conclusion, a
predestined future that she's already aware

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:52.130
of.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:53.850
But this doesn't match up with what we've
seen before.

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:57.469
In the Doctor Strange movie, the Ancient One
presents private doubts as to whether she'll

00:00:57.469 --> 00:01:01.449
even train Strange, confiding in Mordo that
she cannot take him on as a student.

00:01:01.449 --> 00:01:04.709
Mordo has to convince her to train him, which
flatly contradicts Endgame.

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:09.340
"I cannot lead another gifted student to power
only to lose him to the darkness."

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:13.340
It'd make sense for the Ancient One to put
up a front for Strange himself, but why would

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:15.340
she doubt him when he's not even in the room?

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:18.109
It's not magic, just a little plot hole between
movies.

00:01:18.109 --> 00:01:20.299
"I never saw your future.

00:01:20.299 --> 00:01:21.359
Only its possibilities."

00:01:21.359 --> 00:01:22.359
Delivered to your door

00:01:22.359 --> 00:01:26.189
Just as Peter Quill sends the Avengers' plan
off the rails in Infinity War, Nebula nearly

00:01:26.189 --> 00:01:27.950
ruins everything in Endgame.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:32.310
Thanks to her always-on wi-fi, the cyborg
daughter of Thanos is found, captured, and

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:36.270
replaced by her younger counterpart in 2014,
with her past self following the Avengers

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.289
into the future on Thanos' behalf.

00:01:38.289 --> 00:01:42.310
Once there, she flips some switches on the
team's quantum tunnel, targets Thanos' ship,

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:44.289
and opens a portal to bring him through.

00:01:44.289 --> 00:01:48.029
Afterwards, the final battle kicks in so fast
you're left with no time to figure out what

00:01:48.029 --> 00:01:49.499
exactly just happened.

00:01:49.499 --> 00:01:53.100
When the Avengers' Nebula is sent back to
help retrieve the Power Stone, she leaves

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.609
with only enough Pym Particles for a return
trip, particles which 2014 Nebula presumably

00:01:57.609 --> 00:01:58.959
uses in her place.

00:01:58.959 --> 00:02:02.990
She then uses the quantum tunnel to target
Thanos' ship and pull it into the future,

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:06.990
with Thanos' ship somehow able to enter the
Quantum Realm without the help of any Pym

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:07.990
particles or technology.

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:12.320
Also, if it's possible for the 2014 Nebula
to target objects and pull them through time,

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:15.930
why didn't the Avengers do exactly that with
the Infinity Stones themselves?

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:18.879
If Nebula can bring a ship through, why couldn't
they bring some rocks through?

00:02:18.879 --> 00:02:22.720
You can come up with all kinds of solutions
for this, and reportedly, the Russo brothers

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:26.900
have begun explaining in interviews that the
Pym particles were reverse engineered by Thanos

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:27.900
and Ebony Maw.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:32.040
But even if that's the case, the movie doesn't
show any of this, it just starts the explosions

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.219
and crosses its fingers, hoping you won't
think too hard about it.

00:02:35.219 --> 00:02:36.219
Snaps all around

00:02:36.219 --> 00:02:40.060
At the end of Infinity War, Thanos kills half
of the universe with a snap of his fingers.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:43.569
It's a flourish that's ripped straight out
of the comic books, and a gesture that became

00:02:43.569 --> 00:02:44.800
instantly iconic.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.460
But Endgame treats snapping your fingers as
though it's an essential part of the way the

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:51.959
Gauntlet works, so much so that Captain Marvel
is able to stop Thanos from destroying the

00:02:51.959 --> 00:02:55.849
universe during the movie's final battle by
holding onto his fingers, preventing him from

00:02:55.849 --> 00:02:57.209
pulling off a snap.

00:02:57.209 --> 00:03:00.620
But here's the thing, you shouldn't have to
snap your fingers to exert the power of the

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:01.670
Infinity Gauntlet.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:04.300
This isn't how it works in the comics or the
movies.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:08.040
While the Thanos of the movies does usually
close his fist while wielding the Gauntlet's

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.020
powers, it doesn't appear that he really has
to, and the fact that the snap does anything

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:13.209
kind of proves that.

00:03:13.209 --> 00:03:15.819
It just seems like something he does more
for the audience.

00:03:15.819 --> 00:03:19.290
Once Thanos gets the Gauntlet on his hand,
he should be able to rewrite reality with

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.760
little more than sheer willpower, from what
the movies and the comics that inspired them

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:25.749
have shown us, you don't need to snap your
fingers to have it grant your wish.

00:03:25.749 --> 00:03:26.749
Rescue signal

00:03:26.749 --> 00:03:30.530
Relative to all the movies that came before
it, Endgame is like a fire hose of fan-service,

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:34.000
especially the final battle, in which dozens
of characters line up to fight like a living

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.469
comics splash page.

00:03:35.469 --> 00:03:39.720
It's an event that fans have spent years waiting
for, but plenty of people still had to ask,

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:41.830
what exactly is Pepper Potts doing there?

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:45.629
We can readily accept the notion that the
CEO of Stark Industries has become proficient

00:03:45.629 --> 00:03:48.239
at piloting her own Rescue armor over the
years.

00:03:48.239 --> 00:03:51.739
It's not a stretch of the imagination that
she'd learn the ropes of combat off-screen,

00:03:51.739 --> 00:03:55.749
especially after Iron Man 3 saw her vanquish
the villain herself while briefly supercharged

00:03:55.749 --> 00:03:56.969
by Extremis.

00:03:56.969 --> 00:04:00.830
She has as much of a right to fight as anybody
else in the MCU, but the question is, who

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:01.870
called her?

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:06.269
The epic return of all the Avengers post-revival
is implied to have been coordinated by Dr.

00:04:06.269 --> 00:04:10.260
Strange, who got a hold of the other sorcerers
on Earth and immediately set about reuniting

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:12.870
every fighter for a showdown at Avengers HQ.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:16.660
But how did he know to call Pepper, who didn't
suit up in her own Iron Man armor until long

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:18.160
after Strange was dead?

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:19.480
They only met for like a second.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:21.340
Would he even know where she lives?

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:22.460
How'd that phone call go?

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:23.850
"Hey, Pepper, it's Steve.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:27.080
Heading upstate for the final battle, come
through, bring a gun or something."

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:31.090
Whatever the specifics are of who got ahold
of her so fast, we now live in a universe

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:34.930
where Pepper freaking Potts got a shot off
on Thanos, and that's pretty remarkable, whether

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:35.949
you like it or not.

00:04:35.949 --> 00:04:36.949
Renewable resources

00:04:36.949 --> 00:04:39.270
Much is made of the stakes at play in the
Avengers' time heist gambit.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:43.090
On top of playing with forces they don't quite
understand, facing a threat that soundly beat

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:46.479
them before, the Avengers are also dealing
with limited resources.

00:04:46.479 --> 00:04:50.650
As Scott Lang tells everyone, the time travel
trickery of the Quantum Realm relies entirely

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:55.030
on the proprietary Pym Particles invented
by Hank Pym, who in death is unable to create

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:56.110
any more of them.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:59.389
If they use up their supply on the heist without
succeeding, they lose.

00:04:59.389 --> 00:05:02.630
But the answer to this conundrum is right
in front of the Avengers the whole time, and

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:05.699
it's kind of shocking that Scott, a career
thief, doesn't think of it.

00:05:05.699 --> 00:05:09.060
Instead of starting off their journey by trying
to get the stones, why didn't they begin by

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:12.240
going back in time and stealing all of the
Pym Particles they were going to need?

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.419
If the past can be mined for Infinity Stones
and magic hammers, it can certainly be mined

00:05:16.419 --> 00:05:17.610
for Pym Particles.

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:20.970
Even if they use them up and don't return
them, Hank can just invent more.

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:24.610
Sure, it's possible that stealing the particles
and not replacing them will create a slightly

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:28.210
different timeline, but this hardly feels
like a big transgression, considering all

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:31.450
the other maverick improvisation the team
gets up to during the heist.

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:34.759
The only reason the writers didn't take this
route is probably because it would've lowered

00:05:34.759 --> 00:05:35.759
the stakes.

00:05:35.759 --> 00:05:36.759
Captain America's bogus journey

00:05:36.759 --> 00:05:39.580
Everyone's got an opinion about Captain America
going back into the past to live a life with

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:40.699
Peggy Carter.

00:05:40.699 --> 00:05:43.440
Some people love it, while others think it
betrays his character.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:46.889
We're not concerned about any of that, we're
just trying to figure out how Old Cap showed

00:05:46.889 --> 00:05:48.460
up in the prime timeline.

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:50.680
Endgame makes its time travel rules very clear.

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:54.030
It's not Back to the Future style, where changing
the past can change the future.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:55.460
Here's how Professor Hulk explains it:

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:59.389
"If you travel to the past, that past becomes
your future, and your former present becomes

00:05:59.389 --> 00:06:02.280
the past, which can't now be changed by your
new future."

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:05.509
It's all pretty simple, changes to the past
cause different timelines.

00:06:05.509 --> 00:06:09.280
So why and how does Captain America going
into the past in the prime timeline lead to

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:11.740
him growing old and living a life in the prime
timeline?

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:14.919
"The world has changed.

00:06:14.919 --> 00:06:16.840
None of us can go back."

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:21.720
In the MCU so far, Steve Rogers has gone under
the ice, hibernated for nearly 70 years, and

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:25.260
been revived to live a decade or so as one
of Earth's mightiest heroes.

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:29.230
As Endgame closes, he goes back into the past
to catch up on that date he missed with Peggy.

00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:33.699
That past is now his future, a new future,
independent of the one he previously hibernated

00:06:33.699 --> 00:06:34.699
in.

00:06:34.699 --> 00:06:36.210
It should have caused an alternate timeline.

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:39.819
Instead, it's implied that Steve went to the
past of the prime timeline, which completely

00:06:39.819 --> 00:06:41.789
goes against how Hulk says time travel works.

00:06:41.789 --> 00:06:42.789
Whose shield is it, anyway?

00:06:42.789 --> 00:06:45.789
Some people believe that Steve going into
the past and living out his days as Peggy's

00:06:45.789 --> 00:06:49.909
husband is a simple closed loop, that he always
married Peggy, and just stayed off-screen

00:06:49.909 --> 00:06:53.500
during young Steve's reunion with old Peggy
during their meetup in The Winter Soldier.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:57.240
In this theory, there have always been two
Steves, one's just been secretly retired.

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:06.129
"He saved over 1,000 men, including the one
who would become my husband, as it turned

00:07:06.129 --> 00:07:07.129
out."

00:07:07.129 --> 00:07:08.449
That's a weird way to put it, but okay.

00:07:08.449 --> 00:07:11.520
Here's another question, then: Where did Old
Steve get his shield?

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:15.330
Endgame's final battle results in the destruction
of Captain America's shield, leaving it cleaved

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:16.490
in two by Thanos' blade.

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:18.990
He does not take the shield back to the past
with him.

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:22.699
But when Old Steve shows up next to the pond,
he has a shield in pristine condition.

00:07:22.699 --> 00:07:23.699
How?

00:07:23.699 --> 00:07:26.750
If Steve's time-hopping life is a closed loop,
then there should only be one shield to go

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:27.750
around.

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:31.180
If Cap somehow stole his own shield while
his younger self was under the ice, then that

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:34.759
would mean there would be no shield for his
younger self to receive upon waking up, creating

00:07:34.759 --> 00:07:38.930
exactly the kind of paradox that this movie's
time travel rules are supposed to avoid.

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:42.830
So is Old Cap from another timeline, and just
came over to drop a shield off with Falcon?

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:46.389
If so, how'd he get back to the prime timeline
as a 100-something-year-old man?

00:07:46.389 --> 00:07:50.289
Maybe it's not the real Cap at all, maybe
it's just a gigantic plot hole that it'd be

00:07:50.289 --> 00:07:51.759
best to stop thinking about.

